WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
SSO protocols define how identity information moves between systems.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:12.040
SAML is commonly used in enterprise federation, especially for browser-based sign-in to business applications.

3
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:18.380
At a high level, it exchanges signed assertions between an identity provider and a service provider.

4
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:27.040
Those assertions communicate that authentication occurred and may include identity attributes or authorization-related information the application expects.

5
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:34.120
OpenID Connect, often shortened to OIDC, provides an identity layer on top of OAuth-style flows.

6
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:43.580
It is widely used for modern web and mobile applications because it supports identity claims in tokens and fits well with API-based architectures.

7
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:50.800
OAuth itself should be explained carefully: it is primarily a delegated access framework, not a general replacement for authentication.

8
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:56.340
OIDC adds the sign-in and identity layer used for authentication scenarios.

9
00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:02.540
Redirect flows, tokens, assertions, scopes, and claims are protocol building blocks.

10
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:11.120
A user may be redirected to an identity provider, authenticate there, and return to an application with protocol data the application can validate.

11
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.900
Scopes describe requested access or information.

12
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.760
Claims describe facts about the subject or authentication event.

13
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:24.940
Sessions are then established by the application or identity layer based on validated protocol results.

14
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:32.820
Protocol selection should be driven by application type, vendor support, security requirements, maintainability, and operational skill.

15
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:38.760
Teams should understand protocol roles well enough to configure and review them, but they should not hand-roll identity protocols.

16
00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:48.380
Mature libraries, platform integrations, certificate and key management, secure redirect handling, logout behavior, and testing are all part of responsible implementation.
